  <hasPrerequisite rdf:resource="#CITX1001"/>
  <hasPrerequisite rdf:resource="#CITS1001"/>
  <hasPrerequisite rdf:resource="#CITX1401"/>
  <hasUnitCode rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">AUTO4507</hasUnitCode>
  <hasUnitTitle rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Robot Manipulators and Automation</hasUnitTitle>
  <hasUnitLevel rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</hasUnitLevel>
  <hasUnitDescription rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">This unit provides students with specialist knowledge and skills for the design and planning of industrial production, automation processes and the use of robot manipulators. Different techniques and methods are covered, including industrial communication protocols, reliability and safety modelling, sensor and actuator selection and control, and redundancy measures. Control and programming of common robot manipulators is a central part of this unit. Students will learn the theory of manipulators and automation systems and gain practical industrial robot skills from simulation and real robot and automation equipment. The unit further covers industrial application areas, pick-and-place, serial and parallel manipulator types, forward and inverse kinematics, motion equations, homogeneous coordinates and manipulator / automation programming</hasUnitDescription>
  <hasCreditPoints rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">6</hasCreditPoints>
  <hasPrerequisiteText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Enrolment in Bachelor of Engineering (Honours) or an associated Combined Degree and a WAM of at least 50 and  Successful completion of 96 points and ( CITS1001 Software Engineering with Java or CITX1001 Software Engineering with Java or CITS1401 Computational Thinking with Python or CITX1401 Computational Thinking with Python or CITS2401 Computer Analysis and Visualisation )</hasPrerequisiteText>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

