  <hasUnitTitle rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Advanced Reaction Engineering and Catalysts</hasUnitTitle>
  <hasUnitLevel rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">4</hasUnitLevel>
  <hasUnitDescription rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">This unit covers detailed discussion of advanced reactor designs as are employed on process plants. It considers fluidised beds in detail (e.g. as used for waste combustion) as well as covering bioreactors (sewage farms are discussed). It also considers various aspects of catalysis including effectiveness factors, catalyst deactivation and catalyst characterisation, as well as corrosion engineering in the context of undesirable reactions on a chemical processing plant. Reaction engineering is what ultimately differentiates chemical engineering from other engineering disciplines. Here it cements skills in reaction design, looks at more detailed reactor designs involving complex fluid flow or complex kinetics and finally considers catalysts in terms of their characterisation and their longevity.</hasUnitDescription>
  <hasCreditPoints rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#integer">6</hasCreditPoints>
  <hasPrerequisiteText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Enrolment in Bachelor of Engineering (Honours) or an associated Combined Degree and a WAM of at least 50 and  Successful completion of CHPR2007 Heat and Mass Transfer and CHPR3406 Reaction Engineering</hasPrerequisiteText>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

